---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout
  title="The Experiment"
  description="How the CAL DMRadio experiment works: solenoidal LC resonator, SQUID amplifiers, and quantum-limited axion searches."
>

  <!-- Page header -->
  <section class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">CAL-Pathfinder</span>
      <h1>The Experiment</h1>
      <p class="desc">
        Developing and characterizing the lumped-element technology for next-generation axion dark matter searches.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What is DMRadio -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="two-col-60">
        <div>
          <span class="section-label">The DMRadio Program</span>
          <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">What is DMRadio?</h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
            DMRadio is a resonant search for axion dark matter in the sub-&mu;eV mass range. In a strong static magnetic field, the axion-photon coupling modifies Maxwell's equations and produces a tiny effective oscillating current, which DMRadio detects with a coaxial inductive pickup coupled to a tunable LC resonator and a DC SQUID readout chain. Because the targeted axion masses correspond to wavelengths much larger than the detector, the apparatus operates in the lumped-element regime rather than as a conventional cavity haloscope.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">
            Resonant tuning enhances the axion-induced response at a chosen frequency, and scanning that resonance over time allows the experiment to probe a range of possible axion masses. Achieving sensitivity to such weak signals requires high quality factor, careful control of parasitic modes and detector losses, cryogenic operation near 20 mK, and low-noise SQUID amplification.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider">

  <!-- What is CAL-DMRadio -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="two-col-60">
        <div>
          <span class="section-label">CAL's Role</span>
          <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">What is CAL-DMRadio?</h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
            The Cal Axion LC (CAL) experiment develops and characterizes the coaxial pickup and tunable LC resonator architecture used in DMRadio's lumped-element axion search. In this design, the coaxial pickup converts the axion-induced electromagnetic response into a measurable circuit signal, while the tunable LC resonator sets the resonant frequency and provides the narrowband enhancement needed for sensitive readout.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">
            Characterization means measuring not only where the device resonates, but how sharply it resonates, how smoothly it tunes, and whether its electromagnetic field structure matches the intended mode. In practice, this is done through resonance-curve measurements, tuning studies, comparison to simulation, and bead-pull measurements, in which a small perturbing bead is moved through the structure to map the field distribution by tracking shifts in resonant frequency. Together, these measurements reveal distortions, parasitic modes, and other non-ideal effects that determine whether the coaxial resonator is suitable for a precision axion search.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider">

  <!-- How Lumped-Element Works -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="two-col-60">
        <div>
          <span class="section-label">Detection Principle</span>
          <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">How Lumped-Element Detection Works</h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
            In a conventional microwave cavity haloscope, the detector volume must be comparable to
            the photon wavelength at the target axion frequency, which confines cavity-based searches
            to axion masses above roughly 1&nbsp;&mu;eV. In the <em>lumped-element regime</em>&mdash;
            applicable when the target wavelength far exceeds the detector size&mdash;the axion-induced
            electromagnetic signal can instead be treated as a spatially uniform effective current
            driving a simple LC circuit. The detector is characterized by its inductance and capacitance
            rather than by cavity mode structure, so pickup volume and magnet volume can be scaled
            independently of the target frequency. This opens the sub-&mu;eV mass window that cavity
            haloscopes cannot reach.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
            An axion traversing a strong static magnetic field acquires an effective coupling to the
            electromagnetic field, producing a faint oscillating magnetic flux in a nearby inductive
            pickup coil. When the LC resonator is tuned so that its resonant frequency matches the
            axion oscillation frequency (&omega;<sub>a</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;m<sub>a</sub>c<sup>2</sup>/&hbar;),
            the quality factor Q enhances the axion-induced signal power relative to the thermal noise
            floor. Scanning the resonance over time&mdash;via variable capacitors or switched
            elements&mdash;allows the experiment to cover a continuous range of axion masses.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
            CAL Pathfinder implements this concept using a solenoidal superconducting magnet, a coaxial
            inductive pickup surrounding the field volume, and a DC SQUID amplifier chain operating
            near 20&nbsp;mK. The SQUID provides quantum-limited noise performance essential for
            detecting the extraordinarily faint axion-induced currents at the target sensitivity. As a
            pathfinder instrument, CAL validates the lumped-element detection principle at scale,
            characterizes parasitic modes and loss mechanisms in the resonator, and develops the
            cryogenic readout hardware required for larger DMRadio-class experiments.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1rem;">
          <div class="diagram-placeholder">
            <p><strong>Detector schematic</strong></p>
            <p>Coaxial pickup coil + SQUID chain</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider">

  <!-- Setup / hardware -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">Hardware</span>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:2.5rem;">Detector Setup</h2>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;color:var(--blue);">Coaxial Resonator</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">
            The axion signal acts as an effective current source that is inductively coupled to the LC resonator, which is tuned to the axion mass via a tunable capacitor. The tunability of the resonator allows us to search over a broader frequency range.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;color:var(--blue);">SQUID Amplifiers</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">
            The amplifier chain consists of a first stage single dc SQUID, followed by a strongly coupled second stage SQUID array and a cryogenic follow-on RF amplifier. With this system, we can achieve a noise performance that is only a factor of 10-20 above the SQL (Standard Quantum Limit) at the frequencies we are interested in here.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;color:var(--blue);">Cryogenic System</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">
            The CAL experiment will be housed in a Bluefors LD400 dilution refrigerator. The fridge will also be equipped with a 9T solenoidal magnet from American Magnetics, Inc., which is designed to mate with the plates of the LD400 and share vacuum and cryocooling. The targeted system temperature for CAL Pathfinder is 20mK.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;color:var(--blue);">Magnet</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">
            CAL Pathfinder utilizes a solenoid for lumped element detection, this magnet is 9T and will be thermalized at 4K, in the dilution fridge. Axion-photon conversion is driven by the inverse primakoff effect, in which axions are converted to photons and an axion B-field is generated, the quantity we wish to detect.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;color:var(--blue);">Data Acquisition</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">
            The data will be acquired using a modified version of the 'DMRadio Sequencer' code base that synchronizes the data acquisition, frequency tuning, calibrations, and SQUID biasing. The code will need to be adapted to a new digitizer card with the higher sampling rate needed to reach our goal frequency range.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;color:var(--blue);">Analysis Pipeline</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">
            The analysis is based on a frequentist log-likelihood approach. We are presently adapting this analysis method to a resonant search for DMRadio-50 L, including updated vetoes, blinding via fake axion injection, and rescan procedures; the DMRadio-50 L analysis pipeline will be used as the foundation to build the analysis for the CAL experiment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider">

  <!-- References -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">References</span>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Sources</h2>
      <div style="max-width:800px;">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--gray-700);padding-left:1.5rem;">
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
            "scrubbed_Final NSF 45435 Salemi (1)" — Located in the Google Drive under: SalemiGroup &gt; CAL &gt; proposals
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
            "CAL_APS2026_Engelhardt.pdf" — <a href="https://commons.lbl.gov/spaces/CAL/pages/383190220/Past+Conference+Contributions?preview=/383190220/383190219/CAL_APS2026_Engelhardt.pdf" style="color:var(--blue);text-decoration:none;" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LBL Commons</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            arXiv preprint — <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2504.20398v1#S2" style="color:var(--blue);text-decoration:none;" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arXiv:2504.20398v1</a>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="section-sm" style="background:var(--gray-50);border-top:1px solid var(--gray-200);">
    <div class="container" style="text-align:center;">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Want to learn more?</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;max-width:500px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;">
        [Short invitation for visitors to read publications or get in touch]
      </p>
      <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <a href={`${base}/publications/`} class="btn btn-primary">Publications</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
